  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2\\pi =-\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\n- $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\,\u2713$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938136/assets/98324683d6b71e6026341cd70e2c84acb35bb117bd0bb41b627121b3b4f17a79.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
